#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5581" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$, given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1}$.\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - ((-3) \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot (-1) + 2 \\cdot 2)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 2^{2})v_{1}; = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac 7 3\n$$\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "47fadc87-f265-5f1c-827a-28e5be9e8121" :question/math-academy-id "e-5582" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, where $A$ and $\\mathcal B$ are given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A$.\n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1}$.\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - ((-5) \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot (-2) + (-3) \\cdot 2)/(1^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2})v_{1}; = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A)$.\n\nFinally, $a=-4$, $c=-1$, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "60dbc829-01fc-5283-8741-68af483d5313" :question/math-academy-id "e-5583" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$, given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}a & + & b & + & c\\end{vmatrix}$?\n'\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1}$.\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n$= a_{2} - (6 \\cdot 2 + 0 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 2^{2} + (-1)^{2})v_{1}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis an orthonormal basis for $V$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n$$\nb=-\\dfrac1 3\n$$\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & + & b & + & c & = \\left & \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right & + \\left & -\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right & + \\left & \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right\\end{vmatrix} = \\dfrac 5 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e18e9cb-53d3-54c0-8fa5-12ed87e0978e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131847} {:answer/id #uuid "df20fa7e-5d6b-5709-9c33-aaf5e5a5bc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131848} {:answer/id #uuid "e1288fa5-7fb0-5538-a980-97abd0a0c13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131849} {:answer/id #uuid "26860e90-83f5-51a0-8bea-9ee7c9ea797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.ty